{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f07e39d99b57bc5b01", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Ray Optics - Test 5", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923400"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923284", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892321d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "<p>A ray of light passes from a denser medium to a rarer medium at an angle of incidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The reflected and refracted rays make an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> with each other. The angle of reflection and refraction are respectively&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>.</mo></math> The critical angle is given by,</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F34e27a7b-2054-439b-82d4-59617279abdb-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 221px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "28b65a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>cot</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892321e"}, {"id": "5b1e54", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892321f"}, {"id": "486d64", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><msup><mo>tanr</mo><mo>'</mo></msup></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923220"}, {"id": "8f16d3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923221"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923222", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b4f"], "question": {"text": "<p>As shown in the figure, after passing through the medium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>.&nbsp;The speed of light <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> in medium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> will be :<br />(Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo></math>)</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F2db558d4-2c3b-4b82-a15d-b6594ae802ad-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 88px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "e3aa7f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923223"}, {"id": "dc3bde", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923224"}, {"id": "cb6daf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923225"}, {"id": "91fb1e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923226"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923227", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4707"], "question": {"text": "A plano - convex lens (focal length <math><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> , refractive index <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> radius of curvature R) fits exactly into a plano - concave lens (focal length <math><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> refractive index <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> radius of curvature R). Their plane surfaces are parallel to each other. Then, the focal length of the combination will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "de18dd", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923228"}, {"id": "461683", "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923229"}, {"id": "618622", "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892322a"}, {"id": "b7c6c5", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892322b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892322c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "Consider following statements for refraction of light through prism, when angle of deviation is minimum.<br/>(A) The refracted ray inside prism becomes parallel to the base.<br/>(B) Larger angle prisms provide smaller angle of minimum deviation.<br/>(C) Angle of incidence and angle of emergence becomes equal.<br/>(D) There are always two sets of angle of incidence for which deviation will be same except at minimum deviation setting.<br/>(E) Angle of refraction becomes double of prism angle.<br/>Choose the correct answer from the options given below."}, "options": [{"id": "8e52f3", "text": "A, C and D Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892322d"}, {"id": "1a70d7", "text": "B, C and D Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892322e"}, {"id": "02ec17", "text": "A, B and E Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892322f"}, {"id": "94ea1b", "text": "B, D and E Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923230"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923231", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4efc"], "question": {"text": "Car <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> overtakes another car <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> at a relative speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. How fast will the image of car <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> appear to move in the mirror of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> fitted in car <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>, when the car <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> away from the car <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>?"}, "options": [{"id": "18fb8a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923232"}, {"id": "eba4f0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923233"}, {"id": "cb4057", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923234"}, {"id": "144386", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923235"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923236", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e567aa499d1e49588f95c2"], "question": {"text": "An ice cube has a bubble inside. When viewed from one side the apparent distance of the bubble is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. When viewed from the opposite side, the apparent distance of the bubble is observed as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. If the side of the ice cube is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>, the refractive index of the ice cube is&nbsp;"}, "options": [{"id": "09ad89", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923237"}, {"id": "4df1df", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923238"}, {"id": "2d7b54", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923239"}, {"id": "321a76", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892323a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892323b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c2"], "question": {"text": "A mirror is used to produce an image with magnification of $\\frac{1}{4}$. If the distance between object and its image is 40 cm , then the focal length of the mirror is ________"}, "options": [{"id": "7695c9", "text": "10 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892323c"}, {"id": "4055ea", "text": "12.7 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892323d"}, {"id": "a48a0c", "text": "10.7 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892323e"}, {"id": "95f8b0", "text": "15 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892323f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923240", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa023"], "question": {"text": "A transparent film of refractive index, 2.0 is coated on a glass slab of refractive index, 1.45. What is the minimum thickness of transparent film to be coated for the maximum transmission of Green light of wavelength 550 nm . [Assume that the light is incident nearly perpendicular to the glass surface.]"}, "options": [{"id": "a8c3f7", "text": "137.5 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923241"}, {"id": "28ce90", "text": "275 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923242"}, {"id": "cd5e8c", "text": "94.8 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923243"}, {"id": "6a5a02", "text": "68.7 nm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923244"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923245", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "A transparent cube of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math>, made of a material of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, is immersed in a liquid of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math>. A ray is incident on the face <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> at an angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>θ</mi></math> (shown in the figure). Total internal reflection takes place at the point <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></math> on the face <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>.</mo></math><br /><br><br /><br><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F0459979_que.jpg?alt=media\" style=\"width: 160px; height: 143px;\" /><br /><br><br /><br>Then,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>θ</mi></math> must satisfy"}, "options": [{"id": "e67fb8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>θ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&lt;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mfrac><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923246"}, {"id": "aacd9f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#62;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923247"}, {"id": "77deee", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#60;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923248"}, {"id": "70d0cc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#62;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mfrac><msubsup><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923249"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892324a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "The variation of refractive index of a crown glass thin prism with wavelength of the incident light is shown. Which of the following graphs is the correct one, if $D_{m}$ is the angle of minimum deviation?\n\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FBD73f_Y3SKAxOzuwCxcEQiZ8L-l4R4U0R-0cyAZvvG8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">"}, "options": [{"id": "da2efe", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fu0X3hraoHUljvyTHd9OAtXdf3-gC5UZZhFi-LFFNX7U.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892324b"}, {"id": "b15c07", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FTDMWKrOj5fE_-totGE8cuddg4ShbJVU5TjukhZYD74E.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892324c"}, {"id": "5acc36", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FX43058s_fghxcuD-MLcHiznuICgSQOiM3xh92nlHHM8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892324d"}, {"id": "5ee988", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FRJlaxd0jcc7RoRgCganwBGofUJfIyUeMr3AFF8dsg6g.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892324e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892324f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e56813499d1e49588fa38e"], "question": {"text": "A biconvex lens of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> has a focal length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> in air. Its focal length when immersed in a liquid of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></math> will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "c34236", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>16</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923250"}, {"id": "4ff5e2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>160</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923251"}, {"id": "91d4de", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>160</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923252"}, {"id": "9662ec", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923253"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923254", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b565d"], "question": {"text": "What is the lateral shift of a ray refracted through a parallel-sided glass slab of thickness ' $h$ ' in terms of the angle of incidence ' $i$ ' and angle of refraction ' $r$ ', if the glass slab is placed in air medium?"}, "options": [{"id": "3ca412", "text": "$\\frac{h \\tan (i-r)}{\\tan r}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923255"}, {"id": "df9f03", "text": "$\\frac{h \\sin (i-r)}{\\cos r}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923256"}, {"id": "5ef118", "text": "h", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923257"}, {"id": "cca0bc", "text": "$\\frac{h \\cos (i-r)}{\\sin r}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923258"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923259", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4efc"], "question": {"text": "A concave mirror for face viewing has a focal length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0.4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. The distance at which you hold the mirror from your face in order to see your image upright with a magnification of <math><mn>5</mn></math> is"}, "options": [{"id": "6cd47e", "text": "<math><mn>1.60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892325a"}, {"id": "8de525", "text": "<math><mn>0.16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892325b"}, {"id": "e6b67c", "text": "<math><mn>0.32</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892325c"}, {"id": "e6bedd", "text": "<math><mn>0.24</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892325d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892325e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "<p>A ray of light entering from air into a denser medium of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>, as shown in figure.<br />The light ray suffers total internal reflection at the adjacent surface as shown. The maximum value of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math> should be equal to:</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fec5763c4-09fd-430d-bdf4-8d0726b0406d-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 249px; height: 235px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "cc25c4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892325f"}, {"id": "00244b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923260"}, {"id": "d8faaa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923261"}, {"id": "973803", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923262"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923263", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "A monochromatic light is incident at a certain angle on an equilateral triangular prism and suffers minimum deviation.\n\nIf the refractive index of the material of the prism is $\\sqrt{3}$,\n\nthen the angle of incidence is :"}, "options": [{"id": "11ff6a", "text": "$90^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923264"}, {"id": "6548b8", "text": "$30^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923265"}, {"id": "c97be9", "text": "$60^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923266"}, {"id": "4e73bd", "text": "$45^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923267"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923268", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9250"], "question": {"text": "For a concave lens of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi></math>, the relation between object and image distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>u</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo></math> respectively, from its pole can best be represented by (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></math> is the reference line):"}, "options": [{"id": "94ba1b", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F664b0627-406a-4e3f-a88d-05e2d7fd5d18-D637.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 173px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923269"}, {"id": "355f3c", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F30c6f2c5-73d2-42d6-9aa5-7b6f86405f31-D638.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 173px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892326a"}, {"id": "9e7338", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F3a77681c-90d5-4d5e-8c42-318c97c20ff7-D639.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 170px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892326b"}, {"id": "07f9fc", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F6b253189-cd42-4740-827d-18b9dd14dbdd-D640.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 179px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892326c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892326d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b4f"], "question": {"text": "Consider a light ray travelling in air is incident into a medium of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msqrt></math>. The incident angle is twice that of refracting angle. Then, the angle of incidence will be"}, "options": [{"id": "346f46", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892326e"}, {"id": "4bc9ee", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892326f"}, {"id": "308d02", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923270"}, {"id": "ddbdfd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><msqrt><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt></mfenced></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923271"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923272", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c2"], "question": {"text": "A finite size object is placed normal to the principal axis at a distance of 30 cm from a convex mirror of focal length 30 cm. A plane mirror is now placed in such a way that the image produced by both the mirrors coincide with each other. The distance between the two mirrors is :"}, "options": [{"id": "323f1c", "text": "45 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923273"}, {"id": "21f63e", "text": "7.5 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923274"}, {"id": "a1d332", "text": "22.5 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923275"}, {"id": "fcc615", "text": "15 cm", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923276"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923277", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "The refractive index of the material of a glass prism is $\\sqrt{3}$. The angle of minimum deviation is equal to the angle of the prism. What is the angle of the prism?"}, "options": [{"id": "daad4e", "text": "$60^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923278"}, {"id": "8ed6af", "text": "$58^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923279"}, {"id": "5007f1", "text": "$48^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892327a"}, {"id": "7aec66", "text": "$50^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892327b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892327c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["6162fb4c6ed13040ec84d402"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : When an object is placed at the centre of curvature of a concave lens, image is formed at the centre of curvature of the lens on the other side.\nStatement (II) : Concave lens always forms a virtual and erect image.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "04e183", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892327d"}, {"id": "ae6079", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892327e"}, {"id": "db8871", "text": "Statement I is true but Statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a892327f"}, {"id": "195405", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a8923280"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:13.577Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:13.577Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233fe"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233fc", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e0", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["61ad9fc8dedd066c316e4e20"], "question": {"text": "In an experiment with a convex lens. The plot of the image distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup></mfenced></math> against the object distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msup><mi>&#956;</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup></mfenced></math> measured from the focus gives a curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><msup><mi>&#956;</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>225</mn></math>. If all the distances are measured in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>. The magnitude of the focal length of the lens is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "b24174", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e1"}, {"id": "0d4e01", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e2"}, {"id": "0ef9d5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e3"}, {"id": "abb36b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e5", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e1"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a3"], "question": {"text": "<p>Two convex lenses of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> each are placed coaxially with a separation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;between them. The image of the distant object formed by the combination is at _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;from the first lens.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "16ef72", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e6"}, {"id": "fa5f1e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e7"}, {"id": "bef4cb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e8"}, {"id": "5c4acf", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233e9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233ea", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52b6"], "question": {"text": "A prism of angle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#956;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>. A good estimate for the minimum angle of deviation (in degrees) is close to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math> Value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo></math><br />&nbsp;"}, "options": [{"id": "bd877c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233eb"}, {"id": "7e2936", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233ec"}, {"id": "35b714", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233ed"}, {"id": "fd2d08", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233ee"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233ef", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["6176f3f76581241c7072b25c"], "question": {"text": "The image of an object placed in air formed by a convex refracting surface is at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> behind the surface. The image is real and is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> of the distance of the object from the surface. The wavelength of light inside the surface is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> times the wavelength in air. The radius of the curved surface is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>13</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>,&nbsp;the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is&nbsp;______ ."}, "options": [{"id": "71e172", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f0"}, {"id": "db92c2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f1"}, {"id": "f9ae33", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f2"}, {"id": "e803c1", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f4", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["6162fb496ed13040ec84d24f"], "question": {"text": "Two light beams fall on a transparent material block at point 1 and 2 with angle \\(\\theta_1\\) and \\(\\theta_{2^{\\prime}}\\) respectively, as shown in figure. After refraction, the beams intersect at point 3 which is exactly on the interface at other end of the block. Given : the distance between 1 and 2, \\(\\mathrm{d}=4 \\sqrt{3} \\mathrm{~cm}\\) and \\(\\theta_1=\\theta_2=\\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{n}_2}{2 \\mathrm{n}_1}\\right)\\), where refractive index of the block \\(\\mathrm{n}_2\\gt\\) refractive index of the outside medium \\(\\mathrm{n}_1\\), then the thickness of the block is ________ cm.\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FJQWnP_n0Fv3Yrrr47c3WLp4aQUIUqL5IWZb6J79fs78.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>"}, "options": [{"id": "7683a8", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f5"}, {"id": "228afb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f6"}, {"id": "419632", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f7"}, {"id": "1bce80", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f75c677dc36a89233f8"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:27.056Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 5, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:13.677Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:13.677Z", "